+### No lane is a bottleneck: lanes split work into sub-agents
+The user, 2026-10-24: "If one agent per role is insufficient have the agent train sub agents to do the jobs so that none is
+a bottleneck, eg the front end engineer or the uiux agent." So:
+- A lane with a long list (a front-end lane with a dozen layout items, a UX or QA lane covering 30 sizes) splits it. It gives
+  each sub-agent one independent slice: a size class, a component, a test group, a board. The brief names the slice, the
+  files the sub-agent may touch, the checks to run and the output to send back. This uses the Agent tool, where the lane has it.
+- The lane stays the one owner of its files. Sub-agents never edit the same function; the lane reviews and merges their commits,
+  runs its related groups once on the merged result, and sends the lead one tested SHA.
+- Sub-agents count against the usage pacing: every running agent and sub-agent counts toward the cap in the lead's state.
+  Splitting buys speed, not savings.
+- A lane's log `## State` lists its sub-agents and what each owns, so a resumed lane continues them rather than redoing them.
+
 ## Stage 3. Integrate and ship (lead only)
 
 These are the rules learned the hard way on 2026-10-10.
